// weave_spec.md §11/§12: exit(code) terminates the process immediately,
// from anywhere — unlike main's own `return`, which is the *only* other
// way to end the program (§12), exit(...) doesn't require unwinding back
// up to main's own body first.
main = fn(args) {
	// exit(...) bypasses Go's defer/recover machinery entirely (the same
	// way os.Exit itself does) — a recover(...) handler in scope does
	// NOT get a chance to intercept it, unlike an actual panic. This is
	// deliberate: exit(...) always tears the whole process down
	// unconditionally, so there is no fault-isolation concern for it to
	// create an escape hatch around (contrast recover(...)'s restriction
	// inside actor message handlers, §6.5/§20).
	recover(fn(err) {
		print("this handler is never reached by exit(...)")
	})

	print("about to exit with code 5")
	exit(5)

	// Unreachable: exit(...) never returns control to the code that
	// called it.
	print("this line never runs")
	return 0
}
